To run PyTorch natively with GPU acceleration (Metal / MPS backend) on your MacBook Pro M5 Pro, you do not need a special custom build. Modern versions of PyTorch natively support Apple Silicon GPUs.

Note: For training "massive" LLMs locally on Apple Silicon, Apple’s native MLX framework is heavily recommended as it is optimized precisely for this. However, if your codebase requires PyTorch, here are the exact steps to install the working version.


Step 1: Install Xcode Command Line Tools

PyTorch requires Apple's command-line compilation tools to leverage the Metal framework. Open your terminal and run:
bash
xcode-select --install
Use code with caution.
(If it is already installed, your terminal will inform you).

Step 2: Set up a Native Apple Silicon Environment

To prevent architecture conflicts, you must use a native arm64 (Apple Silicon) environment. Using Miniconda or venv with a native Python installation (Python 3.10 to 3.12) is recommended.
If you are using Python's built-in virtual environment, run:
bash
python3 -m venv pytorch_env
source pytorch_env/bin/activate
pip install --upgrade pip
Use code with caution.

Step 3: Install PyTorch

Run the standard installation command. The latest stable release of PyTorch automatically detects and installs the necessary binaries for Apple Silicon GPUs:
bash
pip3 install torch torchvision torchaudio
Use code with caution.

Step 4: Verify Metal (MPS) Acceleration

To verify that PyTorch is using your M5 Pro's GPU instead of the CPU, launch a Python terminal and run the following verification script:
python
import torch

# Check if Metal Performance Shaders (MPS) is available
if torch.backends.mps.is_available():
    mps_device = torch.device("mps")
    x = torch.ones(1, device=mps_device)
    print("Success! Sub-tensor initialized on GPU:", x)
else:
    print("MPS device not found. Check your environment.")
Use code with caution.
If it prints device='mps:0', PyTorch has successfully bound to your M5 Pro's hardware acceleration. When writing your LLM training scripts, ensure you explicitly direct your models and tensors to the GPU by using .to("mps") instead of .to("cuda").
Are you planning to train your LLM completely from scratch, or are you fine-tuning an existing open-source model using frameworks like Hugging Face / LoRA?

